WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.290
I'm Jenna Fisher, and I'm Angela Kinsey.

2
00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:09.170
We were on The Office together, and we're best friends.

3
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:13.270
And now we're doing the ultimate Office Lovers podcast just for you.

4
00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:17.770
Each week, we will dive deeper into the world of The Office with exclusive interviews,

5
00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.970
behind-the-scenes details, and lots of BFF stories.

6
00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.010
We're the Office Lady 6.0.

7
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.580
Hello, everyone.

8
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:28.500
Hi.

9
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.360
Welcome to Office Lady 6.0.

10
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.040
We have a great guest joining us today.

11
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.840
This is one of our most requested guests.

12
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.000
And we have been trying so long.

13
00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.660
There's been lots of scheduling things, and it finally happened.

14
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.800
Today, we are going to be speaking with Lindsay Broad, who played Kathy SiMs.

15
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.340
And she's going to talk all about her time on the show.

16
00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.520
Well, you know, Lindsay joined the show in Season 8.

17
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.780
She was in a total of 12 episodes.

18
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.160
And we all know how much everyone loves Jim and Pam.

19
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.720
So the character of Kathy really made some people prickle.

20
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:03.220
Mm-hmm.

21
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:11.260
But I found an article on Collider, lady, that gave a different perspective on the role of Kathy and what she gave to the show.

22
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:12.860
Oh, I would love to hear that.

23
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.080
It said,

24
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:20.880
Kathy might have gotten between Jim and Pam, but she's a great character because she brings out the best in everyone.

25
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.620
We get to see just how loyal Jim is.

26
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:31.200
The honeymoon stage for Jim and Pam is long over, and the two are now busy every day with kids, yet Jim never wavers once.

27
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.740
And then there's Dwight, who saves Jim without even intending to.

28
00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:43.220
When Dwight comes into the room and sees a half-dressed Kathy with Jim, he doesn't pause or doubt his friend.

29
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.900
He knows Jim would never cheat on Pam, so it doesn't even cross his mind.

30
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:52.440
Bedbugs are truly the only thing he cared about.

31
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:53.340
Hmm.

32
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.780
And a couple of deleted scenes would have revealed even more.

33
00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.580
One has Pam talking to Jim on the phone at the hotel.

34
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.080
Yes, we talk about this in our interview with Lindsay.

35
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.040
We sure do.

36
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:09.000
He tells his wife everything, but rather than being angry, Pam is amused.

37
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:09.740
Mm-hmm.

38
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:18.180
So Kathy's character once again is used to show how strong Jim and Pam's relationship really is.

39
00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:26.800
Well, I wish more people would have had that in mind when they were watching the show, because as we talk about with Lindsay,

40
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.100
man, there were some rough patches of fans really coming for her.

41
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:42.440
Yeah, even the week we interviewed her, she posted on her Instagram a screen grab of a comment someone made where they don't like Kathy, and they think it's her.

42
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:43.940
I know, I know.

43
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.080
So still to this day, she gets it.

44
00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.220
Well, I ran into Lindsay recently in New York.

45
00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.040
We ended up in the same audience of the same play.

46
00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:54.760
I know.

47
00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:56.560
And we talk about that in our interview.

48
00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:04.720
And what it made me realize, Angela, is that I have not shared my big news with everybody, that I am going to be doing a play.

49
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:05.820
Oh my gosh.

50
00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:07.600
I can't believe we haven't talked about this.

51
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:08.640
We have not talked about this.

52
00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.960
You shared, I think, on Instagram, right?

53
00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:12.100
I shared on Instagram.

54
00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.360
But we haven't chatted about it.

55
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.340
Here, this is huge news in the life and life goals of Jenna Fisher.

56
00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.360
Yes, and of Lee Kirk.

57
00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:20.520
Oh yes, of course.

58
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:26.400
This is my second project that I am doing, thanks to Lee Kirk and his amazing writing.

59
00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:34.340
This fall, I am going to be starring in a play called Ashland Avenue, written by Lee Kirk.

60
00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.280
It's going to be at the Goodman Theater in Chicago.

61
00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.160
I mean.

62
00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:39.280
That's a big deal.

63
00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:40.400
The Goodman Theater.

64
00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:41.620
That is a big, big deal.

65
00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.100
It is their 100th anniversary year.

66
00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.720
It's their centennial year.

67
00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.640
We are kicking off their centennial year with this play.

68
00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.040
That's so great.

69
00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:52.860
So please come see me.

70
00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:58.780
Previews start September 6th, and then the play officially opens on September 15th.

71
00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:00.720
I will share all of that.

72
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:01.700
I am coming.

73
00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.620
I can't wait to come and see you on stage, lady.

74
00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.100
I can't wait.

75
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.880
I know how much you love the theater and being part of that community.

76
00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.500
I've gotten to see you on stage twice.

77
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.760
One in Reasons to be Happy, and the second one was Meteor Shower.

78
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:18.900
One was in New York.

79
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.000
One was in San Diego.

80
00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:21.500
Now I'm going to see you in Chicago.

81
00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.260
Lady, you could have a little map with all the little dots of the different theaters you've been in.

82
00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.440
That would be so fun.

83
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:28.560
I have a requeSt.

84
00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:29.140
What?

85
00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.820
Well, then, will you do a play in London that I can come to?

86
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.120
Oh, in the West End?

87
00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:34.620
Yes.

88
00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:35.060
Yes.

89
00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.760
Okay, let's put that out there.

90
00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:38.220
Okay, please, please.

91
00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.160
Well, it's very exciting.

92
00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.400
Very exciting.

93
00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:45.200
I got offered a play in Dublin, and I couldn't do it.

94
00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:48.500
I know, you could have come visited me in Dublin.

95
00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:49.840
That would have been amazing!

96
00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.900
I know, it was a really good play, too, but it just didn't work out in my life.

97
00:04:54.120 --> 00:05:01.320
Well, as our new season and new chapters of our life begin, I will follow you around and go to all of your theater performances.

98
00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:02.960
It's all I want to do now, truly.

99
00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.520
Well, this play, Ashland Avenue, it is funny, and it'll also just break your heart in a million pieces.

100
00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.460
It's a beautiful, beautiful, it's a work of art, and I can't wait.

101
00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.220
Lee is such a good writer.

102
00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:19.240
Yeah, it's got an amazing cast of local Chicago actors, and it's being directed by the artistic director of the Goodman Theater, Susan Booth.

103
00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:20.880
I mean, she's incredible.

104
00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.940
She just directed Helen Hunt in Betrayal, so I can't wait.

105
00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:26.060
This is amazing.

106
00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:26.800
All right.

107
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:32.180
Well, you know, I ran into Lindsay as well, but it was at an office fan convention.

108
00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.420
She made me laugh so hard.

109
00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:35.260
She's so funny.

110
00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.400
She's just so funny, and I loved catching up with her.

111
00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.520
We'll talk about that when she comes on, too.

112
00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:46.760
Well, you all might know Lindsay from her roles in Get Him to the Greek, 21 Jump Street, and Hello, Ladies.

113
00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:52.460
Most recently, you can catch her on the hit show Ghosts on CBS and HBO's Julia.

114
00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:58.300
And today, we dive in with her all about what it was like to play the foil to Jim and Pam's relationship.

115
00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.240
She has a lot to say about it.

116
00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:03.480
So, we'll be right back after this break with Lindsay Broad.

117
00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:12.290
Attention all new and existing members of the WDNC.

118
00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:16.730
I'm Melanie Sanders, and this is the We Do Not Care Club podcaSt.

119
00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:20.950
We got to midlife and realized we just simply do not care much anymore.

120
00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:27.110
Now, we got to help each other make sense of hot flashes, itchy ears, chin hair, trifling families.

121
00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:29.050
Brain fog, and bat wings.

122
00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:31.930
We are not alone, and we will be heard.

123
00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:33.810
So, come join the WDNC.

124
00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:39.210
Follow and listen to the We Do Not Care Club podcast with Melanie Sanders wherever you get your podcaSt.

125
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:44.880
Hi, Lindsay.

126
00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.000
Welcome to Office Ladies 6.0.

127
00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.140
We are so excited to have you here today.

128
00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:50.280
Oh, my God.

129
00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:51.660
I'm glad I finally made it.

130
00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:52.140
I know.

131
00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.700
This has been a long time coming.

132
00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:54.860
I know.

133
00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:58.880
Because my first, when you guys got to my episodes, it was during the actor's strike.

134
00:06:59.200 --> 00:06:59.440
Correct.

135
00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:00.100
Yes.

136
00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:04.080
It's very heartbreaking, because I waited a long time for you guys to get there.

137
00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:06.620
I couldn't believe it.

138
00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.980
Well, we are so excited to dive in, and we're going to start with the question that we ask

139
00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:15.140
all of our guests, which is, how did you get your role on The Office?

140
00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:16.280
Okay.

141
00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:18.440
So, I got my role on The Office.

142
00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:24.040
It was sort of a process, because when they cast the role of Jordan at the end of, I think

143
00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:30.500
it was the seventh season, I had auditioned for that, and I had heard through some secret

144
00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.480
sources that I was one of the choices.

145
00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.120
I didn't end up getting it.

146
00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:42.720
And then, the following summer, I was in Spain with my sister, and for the first time, I brought

147
00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.240
no recording equipment with me.

148
00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:45.940
I brought no makeup with me.

149
00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:51.800
And I got an audition for the role of Carla, which ended up being Kathy.

150
00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.340
And I made a tape.

151
00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:58.200
And my manager at the time was like, I can't send this tape in.

152
00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:02.260
This tape is, he had never done that.

153
00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:03.340
We had worked together for years.

154
00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:05.000
He was like, this tape is so bad.

155
00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:06.460
You're doing nothing.

156
00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:11.340
And also, you're sweaty.

157
00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:12.820
You don't have on.

158
00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:13.900
And I was like, well, yeah, I'm in Spain.

159
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.020
There's no air conditioning.

160
00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:17.340
So, he didn't end up sending my tape in.

161
00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.800
And then, the day I got back, I guess they still hadn't found someone.

162
00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:25.260
So, I went, and I read in person with Allison Jones and Ben.

163
00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:29.820
And it was funny, because I went in, and I did more than I did on my initial tape.

164
00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:37.940
And they brought me down, down, down, until I was doing nothing, aka what I did on my original

165
00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:38.440
tape.

166
00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:39.720
Your instincts.

167
00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:41.040
Yeah.

168
00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.220
And I went to the laundromat, and I was doing all of my laundry.

169
00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:50.600
And I got a text from BJ Novak that just said, yes, with an exclamation point, because

170
00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:51.820
he and I knew each other socially.

171
00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:55.960
And like an hour later, I got a call.

172
00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:57.400
It was like 6 o'clock.

173
00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:59.600
And I had to be on set at 5 a.m.

174
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:00.160
the next morning.

175
00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:00.800
What?

176
00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:01.200
What?

177
00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:02.720
And where were you at the time?

178
00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:04.420
Were you in LA, or were you on the East Coast?

179
00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:05.360
I was in Los Angeles.

180
00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:06.540
Yeah, I was living in Los Angeles.

181
00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:12.580
And I remember I was like, well, I'm going to go get some sushi and take an Ambien and

182
00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:13.160
go to bed.

183
00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:18.440
And I had had an Ambien in my house that I saved for exactly that purpose in case I ever got

184
00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:22.940
a job last minute and knew that I needed to get a good night of sleep, and I was going

185
00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:24.040
to be too anxious to sleep.

186
00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:30.620
I had no idea that we did not cast the part of Kathy until the night before.

187
00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:32.800
I did not know that.

188
00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:33.700
Oh, yeah.

189
00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:39.520
And I had just gotten back from this like trip with my sister, and I was feeling like, you

190
00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:43.540
know when you've been traveling, and I hadn't been on my phone basically for like a week,

191
00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:48.720
and I was like tan, and I was just also in that like travel loopy space.

192
00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:48.900
Yeah.

193
00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:52.980
And I remember I was sharing a trailer with Zach Woods, and he just came up to me.

194
00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:54.640
He was like, how are you doing?

195
00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.560
And I was like, I'm kind of having a nervous breakdown.

196
00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.600
And he was like, yeah, you'll be okay.

197
00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:04.060
I love that Zach was your pep talk guy.

198
00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:05.180
Oh, yeah.

199
00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:05.960
I love Zach.

200
00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:06.380
Yeah.

201
00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:08.660
Had you been a fan of the show before?

202
00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:10.040
Had you watched it?

203
00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:11.800
I had watched all of it.

204
00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:14.000
So you knew all of the storylines?

205
00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:14.580
From the beginning.

206
00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:15.260
Oh, yeah.

207
00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:20.380
I had watched all of it from the beginning, and it was the first time I had ever had a

208
00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:22.280
job on a show that I had watched.

209
00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:25.180
It was very surreal.

210
00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:26.860
It was very surreal.

211
00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:30.740
And what were you told about your character?

212
00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:32.300
Like, you got the job.

213
00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:35.240
You had 12 hours to prepare for this job.

214
00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:36.400
But like, what did you know?

215
00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:39.180
Did you know that you were going to be on multiple episodes?

216
00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:40.300
Did you know?

217
00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:40.820
I knew nothing.

218
00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:45.380
Did you know you were going to be this wedge sort of maybe between Pam and Jim?

219
00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:46.840
So, okay.

220
00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:47.760
So here's what I knew.

221
00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.480
I knew I was hired for one episode.

222
00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:51.080
I knew it had the potential to recur.

223
00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:57.140
And I had absolutely no information other than the fact that the character was described

224
00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:58.280
as Scranton Hot.

225
00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:04.580
So I was like, okay.

226
00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:06.020
And it's funny because…

227
00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:08.820
Please tell me you have a t-shirt that says Scranton Hot, Lindsay.

228
00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:13.600
No, but if somebody wants to make it for me, I'm wearing my t-shirts very large these

229
00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:13.880
days.

230
00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:15.840
I would like a large or an extra large.

231
00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:19.540
I think Scranton Hot is a phenomenal t-shirt idea.

232
00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.120
Isn't that so funny?

233
00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:27.020
I know my husband has been looking for a long time for like a good vintage March Madness t-shirt

234
00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:27.500
for me.

235
00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:28.180
Uh-huh.

236
00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:29.960
But I was like, I can't wear this in public.

237
00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:31.900
But anyway.

238
00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:32.480
Yeah.

239
00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:33.960
So that was all I knew.

240
00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:39.160
And they would basically hire me whatever…

241
00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:41.620
They would hire me at the last possible second for every episode.

242
00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:47.180
So I sort of just would like find out maybe like Friday night that I'd be back again Monday

243
00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:47.420
morning.

244
00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:49.160
I always had a sense that I would be back.

245
00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:51.300
But I never knew…

246
00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:51.760
That is stressful.

247
00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:52.940
That is so stressful.

